
  - [1mImplicit only[0m (you're the creator, assignee, or you commented → you're subscribed). Buys zero UI and
    correct-by-default behavior; new users get notifications without learning anything. Costs: no way to opt out of a
    noisy task you're stuck on, and no way to watch a task you're not attached to.
  - [1mExplicit only[0m (a "Follow" button, nothing automatic). Buys total user control and a trivial data model — one
    [38;5;153msubscriptions[39m table. Costs: empty by default, so the feature looks broken until people discover the button.
    Assignees miss changes to their own tasks.
  - [1mImplicit + explicit override[0m (auto-subscribe on relationship; explicit follow and mute both win over it). Buys the
    right default [3mand[0m an escape hatch. Costs: subscription state is now three-valued (auto / followed / muted), and
    every notification check resolves that precedence. It's the standard model — GitHub, Linear, Jira all landed here,
    which is some evidence it's what people expect.
  - [1mScope-level[0m (follow a list or project, not individual tasks). Buys low-effort broad coverage. Costs: granularity —
    you can't mute the one task flooding you, and it doesn't answer "tasks [3mthey[0m care about" so much as "lists they're
    in."

  I recommend [1mimplicit + explicit override[0m. The three-valued state is genuinely more work, but it's the one choice here
  that's painful to retrofit: adding a mute later means backfilling subscription rows for every implicit relationship
  that was never stored. Getting it wrong in the cheap direction costs a migration.
